       "code": "eigvals = np.linalg.eigvalsh(body.corr().to_numpy())[::-1]\nprint(eigvals.round(3))\nprint((eigvals / eigvals.sum()).round(3))",
       "say": "**Eigenvalues of the correlation matrix** show how many independent \"directions\" of variation the table really has (this is the idea behind **PCA**). Four columns, but one eigenvalue is **0** (to three decimals): the duplicated height. The biggest, 2.83, is the shared \"body size\" direction made of both heights and weight (71% of the variation), then 1.0 for age (25%) and 0.17 for the leftover difference between weight and height. Dividing by their sum gives each direction's **share of the total variation**. When a few eigenvalues hold nearly everything, you can describe the data with far fewer columns without losing much.",
