       "code": "def clean(raw):\n    log = {}\n    d = raw.copy()\n    for col in (\"name\", \"city\"):\n        d[col] = d[col].str.strip().str.title()\n    d[\"age\"] = pd.to_numeric(d[\"age\"], errors=\"coerce\")\n    d[\"spend\"] = pd.to_numeric(d[\"spend\"].str.replace(\",\", \"\"), errors=\"coerce\")\n    d[\"joined\"] = pd.to_datetime(d[\"joined\"], format=\"mixed\", dayfirst=True)\n    log[\"rows in\"] = len(d)\n    d = d.drop_duplicates()\n    log[\"duplicates dropped\"] = log[\"rows in\"] - len(d)\n    bad_age = ~d[\"age\"].between(0, 110) & d[\"age\"].notna()\n    log[\"impossible ages blanked\"] = int(bad_age.sum())\n    d.loc[bad_age, \"age\"] = np.nan\n    log[\"spend filled with the median\"] = int(d[\"spend\"].isna().sum())\n    d[\"spend\"] = d[\"spend\"].fillna(d[\"spend\"].median())\n    log[\"rows out\"] = len(d)\n    return d, log\n\nclean_df, log = clean(raw)\nfor k, v in log.items():\n    print(f\"{k:30s} {v}\")",
       "say": "**One function, one log.** Every rule increments a counter or records a count as it fires, so the cleaning explains itself: 7 rows in, 2 duplicates dropped, 1 impossible age blanked, 1 spend filled, 5 rows out. Because it is a function of the raw table (it never edits it), you can run it again on next month's file and compare the logs: a sudden jump from 2 to 200 duplicates is itself a finding about the data source. Keep the raw file untouched, always.",
