       "code": "def missing_report(frame, warn=0.05, fail=0.4):\n    out = []\n    for c in frame.columns:\n        r = frame[c].isna().mean()\n        out.append((c, round(float(r), 3), \"FAIL\" if r >= fail else \"WARN\" if r >= warn else \"ok\"))\n    return pd.DataFrame(out, columns=[\"column\", \"share missing\", \"status\"])\nbatch = pd.DataFrame({\"id\": range(1000), \"email\": np.where(rng.random(1000) < 0.02, None, \"x@y.com\"), \"income\": np.where(rng.random(1000) < 0.15, np.nan, 1.0), \"referrer\": np.where(rng.random(1000) < 0.6, None, \"ad\")})\nprint(missing_report(batch).to_string(index=False))\nprint(\"a rule you can run on every new batch: names the column, the share and the decision\")",
       "say": "**A missing-data report for every batch.** Missing data is not a one-off cleaning job; in production each new file brings new gaps. A tiny report gives each column a share and a decision: in a sample batch **id** is 0.000 **ok**, **email** 0.015 **ok**, **income** 0.170 **WARN** (above 5%) and **referrer** 0.596 **FAIL** (above 40%). The thresholds are yours and belong in version control with the code. What matters is that the rule runs every time, names the column and the number, and that a FAIL stops the pipeline before a model is retrained on a column that is mostly empty. A sudden jump of a column from 2% to 30% missing is usually a broken upstream system, found hours earlier by a report than by a customer.",
