       "code": "def clean_flags(frame):\n    f = frame.copy()\n    f[\"flag_age\"] = ~f.age.between(5, 110)\n    f[\"flag_price\"] = f.price <= 0\n    q1, q3 = f.price.quantile([.25, .75]); f[\"flag_price_high\"] = f.price > q3 + 3 * (q3 - q1)\n    return f\nflagged = clean_flags(d)\ncols = [c for c in flagged.columns if c.startswith(\"flag_\")]\nprint(flagged[cols].sum().to_dict())\nprint(\"rows with any flag:\", int(flagged[cols].any(axis=1).sum()), \"of\", len(flagged), \"| rows deleted: 0 (the raw values are still there)\")\nuse = flagged[~flagged[[\"flag_age\", \"flag_price\"]].any(axis=1)]\nprint(\"rows used for modelling:\", len(use), \"| the decision is one line you can change, and every row says why it was excluded\")",
       "say": "**Flag, do not delete.** The production pattern is to keep the raw values and add **flag columns** that say why a row is questionable: **flag_age 5**, **flag_price 2**, **flag_price_high 0**. Together **7** of the 1,000 rows carry a flag and **0 rows were deleted**: every raw value is still there. Modelling uses **993** rows by one line of code that filters on the flags, and that line is easy to change, to test (\"does the model change if I keep flagged rows?\") and to explain to a reviewer. Deleting rows destroys the evidence; flagging them keeps the decision visible, reversible and auditable.",
