       "code": "def daily_run(df, expect_rows):\n    clean = run_all(df.copy())\n    lost = 1 - len(clean) / len(df)\n    report = {\"rows_in\": len(df), \"rows_out\": len(clean), \"share_lost\": round(lost, 3), \"age_missing\": round(float(clean.age.isna().mean()), 3)}\n    alerts = []\n    if lost > 0.02: alerts.append(\"more than 2% of rows were dropped\")\n    if abs(len(df) / expect_rows - 1) > .3: alerts.append(\"row count is off by more than 30%\")\n    if report[\"age_missing\"] > .25: alerts.append(\"age is missing too often\")\n    return report, alerts\nprint(daily_run(raw, 3000))\nbroken = raw.copy(); broken.loc[: int(n * .5), \"age\"] = -1; broken = broken.iloc[: int(n * .55)]\nprint(daily_run(broken, 3000))\nprint(\"tomorrow's data is a new test: the run reports what it did and shouts when the numbers move\")",
       "say": "**The daily run report.** Each run returns what it did and a list of alerts. On a normal day: **3,000 rows in, 3,000 out, 0.000 share lost, 0.121 of ages missing**, and **no alerts**. On a broken day (half the ages become -1 and the file is cut to 55% of its rows): **1,650 rows in and out, age missing 0.924**, and two alerts: **\"row count is off by more than 30%\"** and **\"age is missing too often\"**. Wire the alerts to a message and the exit code to the scheduler, and the pipeline tells you before any model is retrained on garbage. Tomorrow's data is a new test: the run must report what it did and shout when the numbers move.",
