       "code": "def fit_auc(frame, target, seed=0):\n    Xtr, Xte, ytr, yte = train_test_split(frame, target, test_size=.3, random_state=seed, stratify=target)\n    m = RandomForestClassifier(n_estimators=150, max_depth=8, random_state=0, n_jobs=1).fit(Xtr, ytr)\n    return roc_auc_score(yte, m.predict_proba(Xte)[:, 1])\nquick = mess.drop_duplicates().assign(spend=lambda t: pd.to_numeric(t.spend.str.replace(\"Rs\", \"\").str.replace(\",\", \"\").str.strip(), errors=\"coerce\"), age=lambda t: pd.to_numeric(t.age, errors=\"coerce\"), seg=lambda t: t.segment.str.strip().str.lower().map({\"new\": 0, \"regular\": 1, \"vip\": 2}))\nquick = quick[[\"age\", \"spend\", \"visits\", \"seg\"]].fillna(0)\nprint(\"quick fix (blanks as 0, odd values kept):  AUC\", round(fit_auc(quick, mess.drop_duplicates().repeat), 3))\nprint(\"careful cleaning (this lesson):            AUC\", round(fit_auc(X, y), 3))\noracle = pd.get_dummies(true.drop(columns=[\"customer\", \"repeat\"]), columns=[\"segment\"], dtype=int)\nprint(\"perfectly clean source data (the ceiling): AUC\", round(fit_auc(oracle, true.repeat), 3))",
       "say": "**What did cleaning buy?** The same random-forest model, scored by AUC on held-out customers: with a **quick fix** (blanks as 0, impossible values kept) **0.714**; with the **careful cleaning** of this lesson **0.723**; with **perfectly clean source data**, the ceiling that no real cleaning can pass, **0.732**. So careful cleaning recovered about half of the gap between the quick fix and the ceiling: a gain of **0.009**, real but small, on a problem where the mess is moderate. Do not oversell it: the gain from cleaning shows up as a better score only when the damage reaches the features the model uses. Its larger value is trust: knowing what each row means and how many were changed.",
